 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes are delivered to browsers over self-hosted WebSocket connections. A browser holds one socket per event it displays; the server pushes each committed revision to that browser's session as the revision is assigned.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed around this choice. The change store retains revisions in replayable order and publishes committed revisions to delivery; delivery owns socket sessions and fans revisions out to subscribed browsers; browsers reconnect after a dropped connection and catch up on the revisions they missed. The project accepts the connection-capacity and reconnect work that self-hosting implies, in exchange for updates arriving without waiting on a poll interval and without a dependency on a managed realtime provider.
 
