 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Self-hosted WebSocket delivery for committed changes.** The server pushes each committed revision to browser sessions over WebSockets it operates. Five-second HTTPS polling was rejected: it adds seconds of lag to every change and makes each browser repeat reads, for a freshness target the socket path meets with room to spare. A hosted realtime service was rejected: it trades a recurring fee and provider coupling for the connection and reconnect work the project is willing to carry itself. The cost of this choice is connection capacity and reconnect handling, which the change store, delivery, and browser-sync nodes must design for.
